  if(p) {
    // 2. Inject the generated HTML template into that new tab
    p.document.write(previewHtml);

    // 3. Close the document stream to signal loading is complete
    p.document.close();

    // 4. Trigger the print dialog as soon as the tab finishes loading
    p.onload = () => p.print();
  }
}}
3. The "PDF Converter" (The Browser)
This is the clever part: The application doesn't actually "make" the PDF.
When p.print() is called:
The browser opens its native Print Dialog.
Modern browsers (Chrome, Safari, Edge) have a built-in destination called "Save as PDF" or "Export to PDF".
Because the HTML template we provided uses @media print styles and fixed A4 dimensions, the browser perfectly converts the webpage into a professional PDF document.
Why this method was chosen:
Zero Server Load: The PDF is generated entirely on your device (client-side).
Perfect Fidelity: It uses the same engine that renders the web, so the fonts and colors look exactly as intended.
Mobile Friendly: It works natively on iPhones (AirPrint/Save to Files) and Android devices without needing extra plugins.
Speed: It is near-instant because it doesn't have to wait for a server to process a file.

